Per Clause [2] and [5]:  
The UE transitions from **EMM-DEREGISTERED.PLMN-SEARCH** to **EMM-DEREGISTERED.NO-CELL-AVAILABLE** when, after an initial intensive PLMN/cell search (e.g., upon power-on or coverage loss), **no suitable or acceptable cell can be selected**, and the UE concludes no cell is available at that time.

While in **NO-CELL-AVAILABLE**, the UE:  
- **Searches for cells at a low rhythm** (Clause [5]);  
- **Offers no EPS services** (Clause [5]);  
- Remains in this substate **until a cell is found**, at which point it exits NO-CELL-AVAILABLE (e.g., to NORMAL-SERVICE or LIMITED-SERVICE) — per Clause [2].

Upon receiving a **SERVICE-LEVEL AUTHENTICATION COMMAND** message, the UE:  
- Stops no timers (unlike EAP-based procedures);  
- **Passes the service-level-AA payload** (from the *Service-level-AA container IE*) **to the upper layers**, using the NAS transport procedure (subclause 5.4.5);  
- Treats the procedure as **transparent to the 5GSM layer** — i.e., no 5GSM-layer processing beyond forwarding.

**Purpose of the overall service-level authentication and authorization (service-level-AA) procedure**:  
To enable the DN (via NEF/UAS-NF) to **authenticate and authorize the upper layers of the UE** — specifically:  
- **During PDU session establishment** (non-emergency only): for initial service-level AA (clauses 6.3.1A.1, 6.3.1A.2);  
- **After PDU session establishment**: for re-authentication or revocation handling (clauses 6.3.1A.1, 6.3.1A.4, 6.3.3.2.1).  

Thus, the correct answer is: **c) both of the above** — i.e., it enables *both authentication and authorization* of the UE’s upper layers, either during or after non-emergency PDU session establishment.
